      "writtenOn": "2026-04-10T17:32:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hmm... For some reason this setting of RAX to 0 is causing Microdroid pVM to hang in pvmfw.\n\nThe symptoms are: when running e.g. `vm run-microdroid -p --enable-earlycon`, pvmfw gets stuck after printing this line:\n```\n[INFO] found a block device of size 28128KB\n```\n\nand then there is 100% CPU consumption on one CPU by the crosvm process, and pKVM vmexit tracing shows that there is no high amount of vmexits, however handling the `vcpu_run` hypercall is taking very long (~12000000 cycles) for some reason. For example, a snapshot of vmexit stats collected during 1s:\n```\n/sys/kernel/debug/pkvm # echo 1 \u003eenable_vmexit_trace ; sleep 1; cat vmexit_trace\nHost-vcpu0: VMCALL 291 cycles 3131758079 each-handler-cycle 10762055\nHost-vcpu0: vcpu_load 28 cycles 5668 each-handler-cycle 202\nHost-vcpu0: vcpu_run 262 cycles 3131749932 each-handler-cycle 11953244\nHost-vcpu0: iommu_domain_map 1 cycles 2479 each-handler-cycle 2479\nHost-vcpu4: CPUID 10 cycles 17372 each-handler-cycle 1737\nHost-vcpu5: VMCALL 82 cycles 1490040 each-handler-cycle 18171\nHost-vcpu5: iommu_domain_map 39 cycles 217504 each-handler-cycle 5577\nHost-vcpu5: iommu_domain_unmap 43 cycles 1272536 each-handler-cycle 29593\nHost-vcpu7: VMCALL 3 cycles 17038 each-handler-cycle 5679\nHost-vcpu7: iommu_domain_map 3 cycles 17038 each-handler-cycle 5679\nHost: CPUID 10 cycles 17372 each-handler-cycle 1737\nHost: VMCALL 376 cycles 3133265157 each-handler-cycle 8333152\nHost: vcpu_load 28 cycles 5668 each-handler-cycle 202\nHost: vcpu_run 262 cycles 3131749932 each-handler-cycle 11953244\nHost: iommu_domain_map 43 cycles 237021 each-handler-cycle 5512\nHost: iommu_domain_unmap 43 cycles 1272536 each-handler-cycle 29593\nVM1-vcpu0: EXTERNAL_INTERRUPT 263 cycles 4745759 each-handler-cycle 18044\nVM1: EXTERNAL_INTERRUPT 263 cycles 4745759 each-handler-cycle 18044\n```\n\nI\u0027ve tried moving this `kvm_rax_write` after the below `kvm_sev_es_mmio_write` just in case, but it doesn\u0027t help. Whereas removing this `kvm_rax_write` (i.e. essentially reverting this patch) fixes the problem.\n\n@jaszczyk@google.com do you have any ideas what may be happening here? Would it be possible that pvmfw, or the libraries used by it, are using the PKVM_GHC_IOWRITE hypercall in some strange way so that they depend on RAX being set to a non-zero value after the hypercall returns?",
      "revId": "ec68b9638cdd7c06a224c81eb4b6a188cf93409e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
